{
  "requirement": "The `mdbook` subcommand MUST act as the mdBook preprocessor for HTML books, rendering requirement definitions and linked reference badges. This project's `book.toml` MUST configure `cargo habeas mdbook` as the `habeas` preprocessor, using a repository-local Cargo alias for `cargo run --release -p habeas --`. Consumer books MUST continue to invoke the installed `habeas mdbook` CLI directly.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The sources satisfy `requirements.cli.mdbook`. The weakest point is test coverage of this repository's own configuration. I judged that acceptable, but it is a judgment call.\n\n**Implementation (all marked `{impl}`)**\n- `.cargo/config.toml:1-3` defines the repository-local alias `habeas = \"run --release -p habeas --\"`. This is exactly the alias the requirement names.\n- `book.toml:6-9` configures `[preprocessor.habeas]` with `command = \"cargo habeas mdbook\"` and `renderers = [\"html\"]`. That meets the requirement that this project's book uses `cargo habeas mdbook` for HTML.\n- `src/main.rs`, in `fn main`, handles `Command::Mdbook { command: None }`. It reads the book from stdin with `mdbook_preprocessor::parse_input`, calls `habeas::preprocess_book`, and writes the book JSON to stdout. That is the preprocessor behavior the subcommand must provide. The `supports` branch returns success only for `html`, which is the HTML-only scope.\n- `src/lib.rs`, in `preprocess_book`, collects the definitions, builds the detail pages, and then rewrites definitions and reference badges in each chapter. It is marked `{impl}` for this requirement as well as the related definition, reference, and index requirements.\n- `src/setup.rs`, in `fn run`, writes `command = \"habeas mdbook\"` plus the `html` renderer into consumer books. That is the \"consumers continue to invoke the installed `habeas mdbook` CLI directly\" half, and it is correctly marked.\n- I found no unmarked code implementing part of the requirement. The behaviors in the marked code are all covered by this or other cited requirements.\n\n**Tests**\n- `tests/setup.rs::setup_is_repeatable` is marked `{test}`. It asserts that setup writes `command = \"habeas mdbook\"` into the consumer `book.toml` and stays idempotent. That covers the consumer half.\n- The preprocessor behavior is exercised by library and integration tests. These include `tests/definitions.rs`, `tests/outcomes.rs`, and the `src/lib.rs` and `src/index.rs` unit tests that call `preprocess_book`. `tests/cli.rs` covers `mdbook supports html` and `mdbook supports epub`.\n- The repository's own `book.toml` and the alias are declarative and have no dedicated unit test. `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs `mdbook build` and `mdbook test`, which run `cargo habeas mdbook` through the alias end to end. A missing alias or a wrong command would therefore fail CI. A dedicated test would be trivial, so its absence is a minor gap, but the CI build covers it in practice.",
